statement: Layer-sensitive residual transition synthesis bound. Let F be any subset of {0,1}^N, and for each t=0,...,N let q_t be the number of distinct residual sets R_t(p)={u in {0,1}^{N-t}: pu in F} over prefixes p of length t. Put S=max(1,ceil(log2(max_t q_t))). There is a deterministic layered membership machine with S state bits and, at each layer t<N, a fan-in-two AND/OR transition circuit with free NOT and constants and shared fan-out using at most 3 q_t S-S gates; its final decoder accepts exactly F. Proof: At layer t assign distinct S-bit codes to the q_t residuals. For each used current code r and input bit b, the next residual is D_b(r)={u:bu in r}; this is well-defined from r and b, so assign it its layer-(t+1) code. For every output bit, construct minterms only for the 2q_t used input rows, at most S AND gates each, for a total of 2q_t S. Among those rows, choose the smaller output polarity set, which has size at most q_t; OR its row indicators with at most q_t-1 gates (zero gates if empty), complement if needed, and sum over S output bits. Total is at most 2q_t S+S(q_t-1)=3q_tS-S. Define transitions arbitrarily on unused state codes. At t=N the residual is {epsilon} iff the input belongs to F and is empty otherwise, so a fixed final decoder decides membership.
scope: All finite binary languages F of fixed word length N; exact layered membership, with layer-specific residual encodings, S=max(1,ceil(log2(max_t q_t))) state bits and the stated per-layer AND/OR update upper bound. This does not establish a lower bound or P!=NP progress.
