statement: Single-output support bound for fan-in-two circuits. In a finite acyclic Boolean circuit whose counted gates each have fan-in at most two, with arbitrary free unary NOT operations and constants, if one designated output depends essentially on k distinct input variables, then its dependency cone contains at least k-1 counted gates. Proof: Retain only the ancestors of the designated output, treating wires and free NOTs as edges and suppressing unary NOT vertices. Let g be the number of counted gates in this cone and let L be the number of distinct input-variable source vertices in it. The underlying undirected dependency graph on these gates and sources is connected because every retained vertex lies on a path to the output cone; adjoining the output wire as a terminal if needed preserves connectedness. Each counted gate contributes at most two incoming edges, so the graph has at most 2g edges after parallel edges are discarded. A connected graph on g+L vertices has at least g+L-1 edges. Thus g+L-1<=2g, so L<=g+1. Since each essential variable must occur among the source vertices, k<=L and g>=k-1.
scope: A single output in a finite fan-in-at-most-two Boolean circuit, with counted binary gates, free NOTs/constants, and unrestricted fan-out. This is a local support lower bound only; it gives no aggregate lower bound for multi-output circuits with shared gates and no Lemma M Pareto conclusion.
