statement: Exact state-width theorem for layered membership. Let N be a nonnegative integer and F subseteq {0,1}^N. For t=0,...,N define R_t(p)={u in {0,1}^{N-t}: pu in F}, and let q_t count the distinct residuals for prefixes p of length t. The minimum number S of state bits for a deterministic one-pass machine with time-dependent transitions, fixed initial state, and an arbitrary final membership decoder is ceil(log2(max_t q_t)). Proof: For the lower bound, if two prefixes at layer t reach the same state, then every suffix produces the same continuation and acceptance result from that state. Hence the prefixes have equal residuals. Thus q_t distinct residuals require at least q_t states, so 2^S >= max_t q_t. For the upper bound, let Q_t be the set of residuals at layer t and assign its members distinct S-bit codes. Initialize with the code of R_0(empty). On input bit b at layer t, map the code of residual r to the code of r_b={u: bu in r}. This is well-defined: if r=R_t(p), then r_b=R_{t+1}(pb), so r_b belongs to Q_{t+1}; it depends only on r and b. Define transitions arbitrarily on unused codes. At layer N the residual is {empty} exactly for accepted words and empty otherwise, so a final decoder recognizes F. The upper and lower bounds agree.
scope: All finite binary languages of fixed length N; layered deterministic machines with arbitrary time-dependent transitions and an arbitrary final decoder. This is a state-width result only and gives no Boolean update gate-cost bound.
