statement: Exact state complexity of a finite layered membership machine. Let F be a nonempty proper subset of {0,1}^N. For each t, let R_t(p)={u in {0,1}^{N-t}: pu in F}, for prefixes p of length t, and let q_t be the number of distinct such residual sets. Among deterministic layered machines with time-dependent state encodings, fixed initial state, an unrestricted final decoder, and transitions required to preserve membership on every input, the minimum number of state values needed at layer t is q_t; hence the minimum uniform state width is ceil(log2(max_t q_t)). Proof: If R_t(p) and R_t(p') differ, choose a suffix u in their symmetric difference. A correct machine cannot be in the same state after p and p', since the common subsequent transitions on u would then yield the same final state and decoder output, contradicting membership difference. Thus at least q_t states are necessary at layer t. Conversely, use the residual sets themselves as states. On input b, transition R to R_b={u:bu in R}; this is well-defined and equals the residual after extending the prefix by b. At t=N, the residual is {epsilon} for accepted strings and empty for rejected strings, so the final decoder distinguishes them. This realizes q_t states at each layer and the stated width. In particular, ceil(log2(|F|+1)) is not the general minimum: only if max_t q_t=|F|+1 does that formula follow. This characterization concerns state count only; it gives no update-gate bound.
scope: All nonempty proper finite binary languages F of fixed length N, deterministic layered membership, arbitrary time-dependent state encodings, unrestricted final decoder; dead prefixes are included through the empty residual when present.
