statement: Live/dead capacity bound. Let F be a nonempty subset of {0,1}^N, and at layer t let q_t be the number of distinct nonempty residual languages R_t(p)={u in {0,1}^{N-t}:pu in F} among prefixes p of length t. If some prefix of length t has empty residual, then every deterministic exact streaming representation that assigns distinct states to distinct live residuals and keeps dead prefixes separate from live prefixes uses at least q_t+1 states at layer t; hence S>=ceil(log2(q_t+1)) state bits. Proof: each of the q_t distinct nonempty residuals is live and must have its own state by the representation's injectivity requirement. A prefix with empty residual is dead and, by the stipulated separation, must have a state outside those q_t live states. Thus at least q_t+1 different states occur. An S-bit state register has only 2^S possible values, so 2^S>=q_t+1, which implies S>=ceil(log2(q_t+1)). This is exact as a layerwise counting statement: if q_t+1<=2^S, counting alone yields no stronger lower bound.
scope: Finite binary languages and the live/dead prefix-separation model in the Lemma M specification. This is only a state-capacity bound; it gives no update-circuit gate lower bound.
