statement: For any finite binary language F of word length N, let q_t be the number of distinct residual sets after prefixes of length t, and let S=max(1,ceil(log2(max_t q_t))). There is a layered membership machine with S state bits and per-layer fan-in-two AND/OR transition circuits, free NOT/constants and shared fan-out, whose layer-t transition uses at most 3q_tS-S gates. Proof: Assign one distinct S-bit code to each residual at each layer. For each of the at most 2q_t reachable pairs (residual code,b), the derivative residual after reading b is determined by the old residual, so its next-layer code is defined consistently. Make a minterm indicator for each reachable pair on the S state bits and b, using S AND gates per pair, for 2q_tS gates. For each output bit, among the 2q_t reachable rows choose the smaller of the rows outputting 1 and the rows outputting 0; this set has size at most q_t. OR its indicators using at most q_t-1 gates when nonempty, then complement the result if the selected rows were the zero rows. Thus at most S(q_t-1) additional gates. Define arbitrary outputs on unreachable pairs. The total is at most 2q_tS+S(q_t-1)=3q_tS-S. At the final layer the residual is either {epsilon} or empty, so a decoder distinguishes acceptance.
scope: Finite binary languages of fixed length N, layer-specific residual encodings, deterministic one-pass membership; no lower bound or P-vs-NP implication.
