statement: For every N>=1 and Boolean function f:{0,1}^N->{0,1}, let E={i: there exist x with f(x) != f(x with coordinate i flipped)}. In the deterministic one-pass layered streaming model with fixed initial state, S Boolean state bits, zero counted update gates (wires, constants, free NOT only), and an unrestricted final decoder, the minimum possible S is exactly |E|. Proof: Inductively, after t updates each state bit is constant or x_i or NOT x_i for some i<=t: this holds initially, and each next wire/constant/NOT is either a previous such bit, its negation, the new input bit, its negation, or a constant. Therefore each final state bit depends on at most one input position. If i in E appeared in no final bit, flipping i would leave the final state, hence decoder output, unchanged, contradiction; so S>=|E|. Conversely, store x_i for i in E at its arrival, preserving the stored bits at every later layer; final decoder evaluates f from these values and fixed values (say zero) on nonessential coordinates. Such fixed values do not affect f by the definition of nonessentiality. This uses |E| state bits and zero update gates.
scope: Exact characterization for Boolean membership functions of fixed input length in the stated zero-gate, one-pass layered model; it does not bound positive gate budgets or prove Lemma M asymptotically.
