statement: Residual-state lower bound for layered membership machines. Let N>=0, F subseteq {0,1}^N, and for each t define R_t(p)={u in {0,1}^{N-t}: pu in F} for p in {0,1}^t; let q_t be the number of distinct such residual sets. Any deterministic layered one-pass machine that decides membership in F, with an arbitrary final decoder and unrestricted time-dependent transitions, must have at least q_t distinct reachable states after t input bits, for every t. Consequently its state width S satisfies 2^S >= max_t q_t. Proof: If prefixes p,p' reach the same state at layer t, then for every suffix u the deterministic transitions from that shared state on u end in the same final state, hence give the same membership answer. Thus pu is in F iff p'u is in F for every u, so R_t(p)=R_t(p'). Therefore distinct residuals require distinct reachable states. The number of available states is at most 2^S, yielding the inequality. The bound is attained as a state-count upper bound by using residual sets themselves as layer-t states and transitioning R to {v: bv in R} on bit b; the final decoder accepts exactly when the residual is {epsilon}. This claim concerns state count only and imposes no circuit-cost bound on transitions.
scope: All finite Boolean languages of fixed length N, deterministic layered membership machines, arbitrary layer-specific transition functions and final decoder. State width lower bound only; no conclusion about G_max, SC_{n,s} witness output, or P versus NP.
