statement: Residual-count growth bound. Let N>=0, F be any subset of {0,1}^N, and q_t the number of distinct residual sets R_t(p)={u in {0,1}^{N-t}: pu in F} over prefixes p of length t. Then q_0<=1, q_N<=2, and for every 0<=t<N, q_{t+1}<=2q_t. Consequently q_t<=2^t and q_t<=2^(2^(N-t)). Proof: There is only one prefix of length 0, so q_0<=1; at length N each residual is either {epsilon} or empty, so q_N<=2. Each residual R at layer t has at most two child residuals, R_b={v: bv in R}, for b in {0,1}. Every residual at layer t+1 arises as one of these children, so q_{t+1}<=2q_t. Induction gives q_t<=2^t. Also every residual is a subset of the suffix universe, which has 2^(N-t) elements, so there are at most 2^(2^(N-t)) residuals. These bounds are exact consequences of the definitions and apply to every finite Boolean language.
scope: All finite Boolean languages F subseteq {0,1}^N. This bounds membership residual counts and hence the state-count lower bound 2^S>=max_t q_t. It gives no transition-gate bound, no witness-output result, and no P-versus-NP conclusion.
